---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Screenshot from '../components/Screenshot.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Layout
  title="Sound & Music"
  description="The sound architecture of Civ 1.5 Remastered: authentic startup audio selection, custom Tandy 1000 SN76496 emulation, AdLib OPL2 FM synthesis, fast 486/YM262 drivers, and Roland MT-32 translated to General MIDI."
>
  <h1>Sound & Music</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Authentic retro sound: from the humble 1-bit PC Speaker, through the newly restored 3-voice Tandy 1000 synthesis,
    AdLib OPL2 FM, official MicroProse 486 and YM262 drivers, to rich Roland MT-32 orchestration and native General MIDI files.
  </p>

  <section class="feature wide" id="audio-selection">
    <div class="feature-text">
      <h2>Authentic DOS Startup & Sound Selection</h2>
      <p>
        When starting Civ 1.5 Remastered, you are greeted by the classic MicroProse hardware setup screens faithfully
        rendered in 640×400 resolution using authentic IBM PC ROM BIOS fonts. The audio menu provides <strong>8 selectable
        sound modes</strong> – spanning vintage DOS drivers, official MicroProse processor patches, and direct General MIDI playback.
      </p>
      <p>
        All original sound drivers from the game (<code>ISOUND.CVL</code>, <code>TSOUND.CVL</code>, <code>ASOUND.CVL</code>,
        <code>ASOUND2.CVL</code>, <code>ASOUND3.CVL</code>, and <code>RSOUND.CVL</code>) are executed by an emulated x86 runtime,
        driving modern software synthesizers and audio pipelines without requiring DOSBox.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-shots">
      <Screenshot
        name="sound-mode-select"
        caption="The authentic DOS sound setup menu in Civ 1.5 Remastered with 8 sound modes."
        image="sound-mode-select.png"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>Available Sound Modes</h2>

  <div class="change-grid">
    <section class="change-card" id="mode-1-none">
      <h3>1) No sounds please</h3>
      <p>
        Completely silences all music and sound effects. Useful if you want to play in absolute silence or listen to your
        own soundtrack in the background.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="mode-2-ibm">
      <h3>2) IBM sounds (PC Speaker)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Driver:</strong> <code>ISOUND.CVL</code> · <strong>Engine:</strong> <code>BuiltInSpeaker.cs</code>
      </p>
      <p>
        The classic 1-bit motherboard beeper. The original DOS driver manipulated the Intel 8253 Programmable Interval
        Timer (PIT) on I/O port <code>0x61</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our engine emulates the PIT timer gate and square-wave frequency generator, outputting smoothed PCM audio at
        44.1 kHz. You get the authentic nostalgic beeps and victory tunes of 1980s and early 90s PCs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="mode-3-tandy">
      <h3>3) Tandy sounds (Tandy 1000 / IBM PCjr)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Driver:</strong> <code>TSOUND.CVL</code> · <strong>Engine:</strong> <code>TandySoundSynthesizer.cs</code>
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Newly built from scratch!</strong> In the original DOS version, Tandy 1000 and PCjr systems produced rich
        3-voice polyphonic music plus a noise channel using the Texas Instruments SN76496 sound chip mapped to I/O port
        <code>0xC0</code>. In early C# translation versions, port <code>0xC0</code> was unhandled, resulting in complete
        silence when selecting Tandy sounds.
      </p>
      <p>
        We engineered a cycle-accurate software emulator of the <strong>TI SN76496</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul class="plain-list">
        <li><strong>3 square-wave tone channels:</strong> 10-bit frequency dividers clocked by the NTSC colorburst clock (3.579545 MHz / 32).</li>
        <li><strong>1 noise channel:</strong> periodic noise and white noise generated by a 15-bit linear feedback shift register (LFSR with polynomial x¹⁵ + x¹⁴ + 1).</li>
        <li><strong>Logarithmic attenuators:</strong> 16 volume levels per channel (-2 dB per step down to silence).</li>
        <li><strong>Exponential smoothing filter:</strong> converts digital chip transitions into clean, warm 44.1 kHz audio without harsh aliasing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Now the charming 3-voice harmonies of Civilization play in full authentic glory!</p>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="mode-4-adlib">
      <h3>4) AdLib / Sound Blaster (OPL2 FM)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Driver:</strong> <code>ASOUND.CVL</code> · <strong>Engine:</strong> Yamaha YM3812 (OPL2) FM Synthesizer
      </p>
      <p>
        The gold standard of PC gaming audio throughout the early 1990s. The game runs MicroProse's <code>ASOUND.CVL</code>
        driver in an emulated 8086 CPU environment, which writes FM synthesis parameters to the 9 channels and 4 operator
        waveforms of the Yamaha YM3812 chip.
      </p>
      <p>
        Delivers the authentic warm brass, synth plucks, and FM percussion that defined the Golden Age of PC gaming.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="mode-5-roland">
      <h3>5) Roland MT-32 MIDI board (Translated to General MIDI)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Driver:</strong> <code>RSOUND.CVL</code> · <strong>Engine:</strong> <code>RolandToGeneralMidi.cs</code>
      </p>
      <p>
        In 1991, the Roland MT-32 multi-timbral sound module was top-tier luxury hardware. It utilized Linear Arithmetic (LA)
        synthesis, custom patch banks, and proprietary Roland System Exclusive (SysEx) messages. Modern operating systems
        do not have native MT-32 hardware, and streaming raw MT-32 commands directly to General MIDI instruments caused
        disharmonious sounds and random drum hits due to mismatched instrument maps.
      </p>
      <p>
        To solve this, we engineered an active real-time MIDI translation engine:
      </p>
      <ul class="plain-list">
        <li><strong>Dynamic timbre mapping:</strong> maps MT-32 preset instrument numbers to their closest General MIDI acoustic and orchestral equivalents.</li>
        <li><strong>Percussion remapping & redirection:</strong> intercepts MT-32 rhythm note triggers and routes them cleanly to General MIDI Channel 10 with calibrated note offsets.</li>
        <li><strong>SysEx sanitation & controller management:</strong> strips hardware-specific SysEx commands while preserving pitch bend, volume expressions, and channel pans. Emits automated "All Notes Off" commands to eliminate stuck notes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Music is played through your OS default MIDI synthesizer (such as the Microsoft GS Wavetable Synth), producing
        rich cinematic renditions of every historical civilization theme.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="mode-6-custom">
      <h3>6) Custom sound driver (Smart Auto-Detection)</h3>
      <p>
        The default mode when launching the game. It dynamically queries your operating system and audio subsystem,
        selecting the best available audio output according to priority:
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Roland MT-32 (via General MIDI) → AdLib FM → PC Speaker</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        Guarantees optimal sound quality out of the box with zero manual configuration.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="mode-7-fast-adlib">
      <h3>7) AdLib (Fast 486 / YM262 fix)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Drivers:</strong> <code>ASOUND2.CVL</code> / <code>ASOUND3.CVL</code> · <strong>Author:</strong> Jim McConkey (MicroProse, 1994)
      </p>
      <p>
        Official MicroProse update combining high-speed execution patches with advanced Yamaha YM262 chip fixes:
      </p>
      <ul class="plain-list">
        <li><strong>High-Speed 486 / Pentium Delay Patch:</strong> On fast CPUs, the original <code>ASOUND.CVL</code> I/O port delay loops ran too quickly, causing dropped notes. This driver incorporates calibrated cycle delays for seamless playback.</li>
        <li><strong>Yamaha YM262 (OPL-3) Stereo Fix:</strong> Fixes 4-operator register and stereo initialization on Sound Blaster 16 and Pro 2 hardware.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="change-card" id="mode-8-midi">
      <h3>8) General MIDI (.MID files)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Assets:</strong> 18 Standard MIDI files (located in <code>Assets/Midi/</code>) · <strong>Engine:</strong> <code>GeneralMidiPlayer.cs</code>
      </p>
      <p>
        Direct, high-fidelity playback of authentic Standard MIDI (.MID) arrangements crafted specifically for Civilization.
        The full library includes all 18 original soundtrack compositions:
      </p>
      <ul class="plain-list">
        <li><code>opening_raw.mid</code> – the iconic title theme from the main menu.</li>
        <li><code>evolution_raw.mid</code> – the dawn of mankind creation sequence.</li>
        <li><code>victory_raw.mid</code> and <code>defeat_raw.mid</code> – fanfare of triumph and solemn defeat.</li>
        <li><strong>14 civilization anthems:</strong> Americans, Aztecs, Babylonians, Chinese, Egyptians, English, French, Germans, Greeks, Indians, Mongols, Romans, Russians, and Zulus.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Audio is driven directly by the OS multimedia MCI sequencer, delivering crisp, pristine instrumental arrangements.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2>Audio Architecture Comparison</h2>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Mode</th>
          <th>Original Driver</th>
          <th>Target Hardware</th>
          <th>Implementation in Civ 1.5</th>
          <th>Acoustic Character</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>1) None</strong></td>
          <td>—</td>
          <td>Silence</td>
          <td>Muted pipeline</td>
          <td>Complete silence</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>2) IBM PC</strong></td>
          <td><code>ISOUND.CVL</code></td>
          <td>Intel 8253 PIT / Port 0x61</td>
          <td>Software square-wave PIT synthesis</td>
          <td>Iconic 1-bit retro beep tones</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>3) Tandy 1000</strong></td>
          <td><code>TSOUND.CVL</code></td>
          <td>TI SN76496 / Port 0xC0</td>
          <td>Custom SN76496 hardware emulator</td>
          <td>Warm 3-voice polyphony + noise percussion</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>4) AdLib / SB</strong></td>
          <td><code>ASOUND.CVL</code></td>
          <td>Yamaha YM3812 (OPL2)</td>
          <td>Software OPL2 FM synthesis emulation</td>
          <td>Crisp 9-channel FM synth sounds</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>5) Roland MT-32</strong></td>
          <td><code>RSOUND.CVL</code></td>
          <td>Roland MT-32 LA Synth</td>
          <td><code>RolandToGeneralMidi</code> translator</td>
          <td>Rich multi-timbral General MIDI orchestration</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>6) Custom Driver</strong></td>
          <td>Auto-detected</td>
          <td>Best available</td>
          <td>Intelligent fallback chain</td>
          <td>Highest available fidelity out of the box</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>7) Fast 486 / YM262</strong></td>
          <td><code>ASOUND2.CVL</code> / <code>ASOUND3.CVL</code></td>
          <td>AdLib on fast 486/Pentium CPUs / OPL-3</td>
          <td>Jim McConkey delay patch (1994)</td>
          <td>No desynchronization and stable OPL-3 registers</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>8) General MIDI</strong></td>
          <td>18 <code>.MID</code> files</td>
          <td>Standard General MIDI (GM)</td>
          <td>Native <code>GeneralMidiPlayer</code></td>
          <td>Direct, lossless studio MIDI playback</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</Layout>
